WEBVTT

00:00:00.285 --> 00:00:03.836
Both versions of the Orders API return 201.

00:00:04.006 --> 00:00:09.422
The order identifiers and timestamps change
every time, so a raw comparison makes it

00:00:09.422 --> 00:00:11.421
hard to spot the changed total.

00:00:11.985 --> 00:00:15.743
Save the baseline and candidate traffic to
separate folders.

00:00:15.914 --> 00:00:20.285
In this example, each folder contains three
successful order responses.

00:00:20.456 --> 00:00:22.124
The orders are the same size.

00:00:22.685 --> 00:00:27.830
Run proxymock automation response-diff with
the baseline and candidate folders.

00:00:28.001 --> 00:00:30.850
Add -o pretty to read the result in the
terminal.

00:00:31.021 --> 00:00:35.689
The command pairs responses and filters
fields it recognizes as volatile.

00:00:36.252 --> 00:00:39.669
The report shows one finding across three
compared responses.

00:00:39.839 --> 00:00:43.816
Total cents changed from 3,600 to 4,800.

00:00:43.987 --> 00:00:48.320
The generated identifiers and timestamps
don't appear as regressions.

00:00:48.885 --> 00:00:53.401
Follow the method, endpoint, and field path
to the exact response value.

00:00:53.572 --> 00:00:58.661
Check whether the new total is intentional,
then use the same baseline after a fix.

00:00:59.219 --> 00:01:03.764
Save your baseline so you can run the
comparison again when the service changes.
